{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>The peoples heard, they trembled</b>. He says: when the peoples heard, they trembled from the might of Your hand, and terror seized the inhabitants of Philistia at the time they heard. And it is possible that he says that the peoples had already heard all that HaShem had done in the land of Egypt and they tremble continually from the plague that He brought upon them, and he prays that terror and dread fall upon them so that they do not go out to meet Israel for battle. And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 15:16) said that <b>it falls upon them</b> refers back to Philistia, Edom, and Moab, not to Canaan, and this is why it says <b>until Your people pass, O YHWH</b>, for Israel passed through their territories before coming to the land of Canaan, and did not wage war against them. And even though Edom came out to meet them with a heavy people and with a strong hand (Numbers 20:20), to prevent them from passing through his land, he did not wage war against them; and had terror and dread not fallen upon them, they would have wanted to wage war against them out of their hatred for them. And he did not mention Ammon, because Ammon and Moab are like a single nation. And it is possible that fear of them also fell upon the Canaanite and he did not wage war against them until they had passed through, for the Canaanite king of Arad who dwells in the Negev (Numbers 21:1) is not a Canaanite by lineage, according to the opinion of our Sages (Numbers Rabbah and Tanhuma ad loc.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:39.005921992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}